==================================================================
DAY 44 - PART A: THEORY
How a Managed Cluster Is Structured
Time: 30-40 minutes
==================================================================

1. WHAT eksctl CREATES
----------------------
Two CloudFormation stacks:
  eksctl-lab-cluster        VPC, subnets (public + private in each AZ),
                            Internet Gateway, NAT gateway, route tables,
                            security groups, IAM role for the control
                            plane, the EKS cluster itself
  eksctl-lab-nodegroup-ng-1 IAM role for nodes, launch template,
                            Auto Scaling Group of EC2 instances
Plus add-ons (vpc-cni, coredns, kube-proxy, pod identity agent, EBS
CSI) and an OIDC provider for IRSA.


2. CONTROL PLANE YOU CAN'T SEE
------------------------------
The API servers and etcd run in an AWS-owned account across 3 AZs.
You get an HTTPS endpoint. "k get pods -n kube-system" shows NO
apiserver/etcd/scheduler pods, only node-level add-ons.


3. NODE GROUPS
--------------
Managed node group = EKS-managed Auto Scaling Group of EC2 instances
running an EKS-optimized OS (Amazon Linux 2023 or Bottlerocket).
min/desired/max size; upgrades by replacing nodes with drain.
Alternatives:
  Self-managed nodes   your own ASG, full control
  Fargate              serverless pods (no nodes to manage, limits)
  EKS Auto Mode        AWS manages nodes, scaling, core add-ons
  Karpenter            provisions right-sized nodes on demand
GKE Autopilot and AKS Automatic are similar "nodeless" modes.


4. KUBECONFIG FOR EKS
---------------------
eksctl (or "aws eks update-kubeconfig") adds a context whose user
runs "aws eks get-token". Your IAM identity becomes your Kubernetes
identity. The creator gets cluster-admin via an "access entry".


5. MULTI-AZ
-----------
Nodes are spread over AZs; each node has the label
topology.kubernetes.io/zone. Use topologySpreadConstraints (Week 5)
to spread replicas across zones for high availability.


KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
----------------------
- eksctl = CloudFormation stacks: cluster/VPC + node group.
- No control plane pods visible.
- Access = IAM identity -> access entry -> Kubernetes permissions.
